{"attempt_id": "attempt:um-ep-996-erd-s-problem-996-12ef0c72-source-fidelity-01", "created_at": "2026-09-06T08:00:00Z", "graph_id": "graph:um-ep-996-erd-s-problem-996-12ef0c72-v1", "obligations": [{"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof", "counterexample", "formalization", "computation"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": ["obligation:um-ep-996-erd-s-problem-996-12ef0c72-statement-fidelity"], "kind": "root_claim", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-ep-996-erd-s-problem-996-12ef0c72-root", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/EP-996"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Let $n_1<n_2<\\cdots$ be a lacunary sequence of integers, and let $f\\in L^2([0,1])$. Let $f_n$ be the $n$th partial sum of the Fourier series of $f(x)$. Is there an absolute constant $C>0$ such that, if $ \\| f-f_n\\|_2 \\ll \\frac{1}{(\\log\\log\\log n)^{C}} $ then $ \\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{k\\leq N}f(\\{\\alpha n_k\\})=\\int_0^1 f(x)\\mathrm{d}x $ for almost every $\\alpha$?"}, "statement_sha256": "c4af7fe267b92ca2dd2f3386d1aa15c3c0811e167ff1af4e62f00bbc15def412"}, {"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": [], "kind": "definition", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-ep-996-erd-s-problem-996-12ef0c72-statement-fidelity", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/EP-996"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Verify against the cited source that the frozen statement is complete, current, untruncated, attribution-correct, and unambiguous enough for mathematical research; record any definition or quantifier gap without silently editing the contract."}, "statement_sha256": "65f0b3d574bd6d837403149dab6434c0f571709f7eb5bb066c7e8774bad13f34"}], "problem_contract_sha256": "d556a5f82dfcd568723de5540c6747062d36d6110215b3125bce29ce9e3409cd", "probl